WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.597
In this scene in Universal's 2020 film

00:00:04.597 --> 00:00:07.140
"The Invisible Man,"
Elisabeth Moss' character

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.173
is thrown across the dining-room
table by the monster.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.700
Nailing this shot wasn't easy.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.130
It involved two stunt performers,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.662
one of whom was in a green suit;

00:00:15.662 --> 00:00:17.393
various ropes; a VFX team;

00:00:17.393 --> 00:00:18.993
and the precise movements of a

00:00:18.993 --> 00:00:21.620
robotic, motion-controlled camera.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.540
Now compare that to
the original 1933 film.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.380
The main character is also "invisible,"

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.190
and while they did use
some practical effects

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.420
to move objects around,
for most of the movie,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.290
he's simply covered in bandages.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.765
The new movie couldn't rely on just

00:00:33.765 --> 00:00:36.290
hidden strings and
bandages for a cheap scare.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.881
It shakes you to the core by using

00:00:37.881 --> 00:00:40.320
innovative effects and
smart filmmaking techniques

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.700
to make the audience terrified

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.370
of something they can't even see.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.310
An upgrade to a classic monster

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.140
that is much more real and
terrifying this time around.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.200
Now, warning: spoilers ahead
if you haven't seen the movie.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.340
To see how far we've
come, we have to look back

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.820
to 1933 and to this man, Claude Rains.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:58.978
He's the actor who played

00:00:58.978 --> 00:01:00.703
the invisible man in that film.

00:01:00.703 --> 00:01:02.830
To create the illusion
that Rains was invisible,

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.280
the filmmakers wrapped
parts of him in black velvet

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.430
and filmed him in front
of a black background.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:08.760
This made whatever part of his body

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.118
that was supposed to be invisible

00:01:10.118 --> 00:01:11.442
seem to disappear.

00:01:11.442 --> 00:01:14.774
Officer: Look, he's all eaten away.

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.140
Narrator: Otherwise,
the rest of his clothes

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.480
appeared to float in the air,

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.430
a gimmicky look they tried
to avoid in the new film.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.615
For the 2020 remake, they decided to make

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.510
the invisible man, Adrian Griffin,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.530
completely invisible, making him

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.860
a more mysterious and unknown figure.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.410
And thanks to technological advances

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.418
in the decades between the two films,

00:01:32.418 --> 00:01:33.443
they could do it.

00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:35.570
But Elisabeth Moss and
the rest of the cast

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.700
still had to have something to react to.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.576
This required a stunt
performer in a green suit.

00:01:40.576 --> 00:01:42.220
The stuntman would help guide the actors

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.920
and interact with them
for the fight scenes,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.130
like the one in the kitchen.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.693
They used a robotic camera rig

00:01:46.693 --> 00:01:48.870
that could be programmed
to shoot each take

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.440
with the exact same timing and movement.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:52.600
This allowed them to capture

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.120
both the take with the stuntman

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.870
and one clean take without him.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.730
Editors could then merge the two shots

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.940
and digitally erase him afterwards.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.260
Let's look at that kitchen scene again

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.840
as an example of how this
technique would work.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.273
First, they would film Moss

00:02:05.273 --> 00:02:07.073
as she was about to be thrown.

00:02:07.073 --> 00:02:09.550
Then they would swap her
out with a stuntwoman.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.650
She would be attached
to a rope for support,

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.180
and the stuntman in the green suit

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.170
would throw her across the table.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.160
They would swap in Moss again

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.740
and stitch the three shots together,

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.743
removing the stuntman
and rope from the clip.

00:02:21.743 --> 00:02:23.610
Removing an actor in
a green suit, however,

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.026
was difficult to do perfectly

00:02:25.026 --> 00:02:26.809
with so many moving parts.

00:02:26.809 --> 00:02:28.695
So they also digitally reconstructed

00:02:28.695 --> 00:02:30.960
some of the background to
make the final shot cleaner.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.990
As director Leigh Whannell
said in an interview

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.470
with "The Big Picture" podcast,

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.110
"It's actually easier to
add in a werewolf later

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.852
than it is to subtract a stunt
performer in a green suit."

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.960
By rendering him completely invisible,

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.120
it was up to the audience
to guess where he was,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.590
without a floating hat or
sunglasses to guide them.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.520
And when he made himself
known through violence,

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.103
it felt more like a real person

00:02:51.103 --> 00:02:52.536
was actually there attacking,

00:02:52.536 --> 00:02:54.930
which made the new film
much more unsettling

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.800
than the original.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.150
But not every scene required
the actor in a green suit.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.080
Some of the most tension-filled moments

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.087
were the ambiguous ones, when the audience

00:03:03.087 --> 00:03:04.830
doesn't know if Adrian is there or not.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.714
This required some clever cinematography.

00:03:07.714 --> 00:03:09.088
One way they made the invisible man

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.330
seem so real without using any VFX

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:13.950
was through frequent,
calculated panning shots.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.970
In most films, regardless of the genre,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.350
the camera pans to a
specific character or object

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.645
to focus the audience's attention.

00:03:20.645 --> 00:03:22.360
Take a look at this scene
from "The Revenant."

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.870
The camera pans to the left

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.100
to show the bear approaching
Hugh from the woods.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.630
And in "The Shining," while
Danny is hiding in the maze,

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.257
we see the camera pan to the path,

00:03:30.257 --> 00:03:33.530
showing his father
limping around with an ax.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:35.425
We as the audience have come to expect

00:03:35.425 --> 00:03:39.310
someone or something to be
in this space after a pan.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.170
But "The Invisible Man"
subverts our expectations

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.510
and pans to nothingness.
Just an empty space.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.601
When the camera stops,

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:48.642
Adrian should be there, but he isn't.

00:03:48.642 --> 00:03:51.410
And this creates a sense of
uneasiness for the viewer,

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.210
putting us in the mind of Cecilia,

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:56.008
where we also feel haunted
by an invisible threat.

00:03:57.228 --> 00:03:59.430
The other key to making
the cinematography scary

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.605
was to use the negative space.

00:04:01.605 --> 00:04:03.590
Basically, they filmed
lots of empty spaces

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:04.960
where the character might be,

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.650
even though you can't actually see him,

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.700
and the audience is never quite sure.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.970
Much of this was accomplished
through unusual framing,

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.660
leaving extra space
around or behind Cecilia.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:15.700
You assume he's standing in the corner

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.991
or sitting in the chair,

00:04:16.991 --> 00:04:18.410
which makes the audience uneasy.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.500
For example, look at
the interrogation scene.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.350
The corner of the room
is bare and in frame.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.507
Cecilia and the audience
assume he's standing

00:04:25.507 --> 00:04:27.970
right there in the shot,
listening to everything.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.490
One of the kitchen scenes
is a wide still shot.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.048
It lingers there for a long time

00:04:32.048 --> 00:04:33.908
after Cecilia leaves the frame.

00:04:33.908 --> 00:04:35.447
This was intended to be a moment

00:04:35.447 --> 00:04:37.553
for the audience to
frantically search the image

00:04:37.553 --> 00:04:39.165
for any signs of Adrian.

00:04:39.165 --> 00:04:40.990
Whannell says he wanted you to be afraid

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.110
you would miss something.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.833
The director confirmed
that the invisible man

00:04:43.833 --> 00:04:45.120
is in most of those shots,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.750
but only he knows exactly which ones.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.215
And there are only a
few where the character

00:04:49.215 --> 00:04:51.641
is not present where we think he might be.

00:04:51.641 --> 00:04:54.220
Aside from using the camera
to film negative spaces,

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.660
they also used it as if it
was a character of its own

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.440
at certain points in the movie.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.560
The same robotic, motion-controlled camera

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.750
used for the kitchen
scene we mentioned earlier

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.762
had precise, controlled
movements, just like Adrian,

00:05:05.762 --> 00:05:07.500
since the camera represents his presence

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.960
in a lot of the movie.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.450
For example, in the hallway scene

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:11.930
at the psychiatric hospital,

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.910
watch as it quickly darts back and forth

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.530
and even bends to the side.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.800
You feel like you're looking
through Adrian's eyes

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.070
as he goes on his murder spree.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.140
It has quicker movements
than handheld cameras,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.855
with odd, jarring angles.

00:05:24.855 --> 00:05:26.790
Using a robotic camera meant that actors

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.030
had to get their timing right

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:29.500
and hit their marks perfectly,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.830
because the camera doesn't stop.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.390
It's programmed to go through a specific

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:34.386
set of motions at a specific pace.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:37.035
By combining all of these elements,

00:05:37.035 --> 00:05:39.680
Whannell created a believable,
terrifying character

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.610
that we think is on screen
but hardly ever see,

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.470
without requiring any gimmicky bandages

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.697
or clichéd, spooky, dark sets.

00:05:46.697 --> 00:05:48.200
"The Invisible Man" managed to turn

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.980
a small $7 million
budget into a big scare.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.440
And it worked, ultimately,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.130
because it's often what isn't there

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:55.730
that can scare you the most.

